<td><div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="description en" style="display:inline;"><i>This work is in the <b><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/public_domain" class="extiw" title="w:public domain">public domain</a></b> because it was published in the United States between 1930 and 1963, and although there may or may not have been a copyright notice, the <b><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copyright_renewal_in_the_United_States" class="extiw" title="w:Copyright renewal in the United States">copyright was not renewed</a></b>. For further explanation, see <a href="/wiki/Commons:Hirtle_chart" title="Commons:Hirtle chart">Commons:Hirtle chart</a> and <a rel="nofollow" class="external text" href="http://onlinebooks.library.upenn.edu/cce/">the copyright renewal logs</a>. <div class="navigation-not-searchable">Note that it may still be copyrighted in jurisdictions that do not apply the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/rule_of_the_shorter_term" class="extiw" title="w:rule of the shorter term">rule of the shorter term</a> for US works (depending on the date of the author's death), such as Canada (70 years <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Latin_phrases_(P)#Post_mortem_auctoris" class="extiw" title="w:List of Latin phrases (P)">p.m.a.</a>), Mainland China (50 years p.m.a., not Hong Kong or Macao), Germany (70 years p.m.a.), Mexico (100 years p.m.a.), Switzerland (70 years p.m.a.), and other countries with individual treaties.</div></i></div>
<hr>
